In terms of parsing and manipulating JSON data in Go, there are several libraries available. The popular ones include encoding/json, go-json-simple, and jsoncpp. These libraries provide functions to encode and decode JSON data, as well as to access specific fields within a JSON object.
Here's an example of how to use encoding/json library in Go to encode and decode JSON data:
```go
package main
import (
    "encoding/json"
    "fmt"
)
type Person struct {
    Name string `json:"name"`
    Age int `json:"age"`
}
func main() {
    person := &Person{
        Name: "John",
        Age: 30,
    }
    jsonData, err := json.Marshal(person)
    if err != nil {
        fmt.Println(err)
        return
    }
    fmt.Println(string(jsonData))
    var decodedPerson *Person
    err = json.Unmarshal(jsonData, &decodedPerson)
    if err != nil {
        fmt.Println(err)
        return
    }
    fmt.Println(decodedPerson.Name, decodedPerson.Age)
}
```
This code defines a Person struct and encodes it as JSON data using the Marshal function. It then unmarshals the JSON data back into a Person struct using the Unmarshal function. Finally, it prints out the name and age of the person.

